The two things that needed care:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe [38;5;10mgoto[0m control flow in [38;5;10mfoo[0m.[22m Both [38;5;10mlabel1[0m and [38;5;10mlabel2[0m sit inside the [38;5;10mwhile[0m body, so [38;5;10mgoto label1[0m re-enters the body [3mwithout[23m re-testing the loop condition, while [38;5;10mcontinue[0m jumps back to the condition. I modeled this as an inner [38;5;10mloop[0m acting as the label region, with a [38;5;10mentry_at_label1[0m flag choosing whether control lands on [38;5;10mlabel1[0m or falls straight through to [38;5;10mlabel2[0m (the [38;5;10mx == 1 && y == 4[0m case). [38;5;10mcontinue 'while_loop[0m handles the [38;5;10my == 0[0m case. Decrements use [38;5;10mwrapping_sub[0m so the negative-[38;5;10my[0m runaway path wraps like the C build instead of panicking.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mscanf("%d %d", ...)[0m.[22m Implemented a byte-level reader with single-byte pushback so whitespace-skipping crosses newlines the way [38;5;10mscanf[0m does. [38;5;10mx[0m and [38;5;10my[0m stay at their initialized [38;5;10m0[0m when a conversion fails, and the second variable is left untouched if the first fails — matching C, where a partial match like [38;5;10m5 abc[0m leaves [38;5;10my == 0[0m. Overflow follows glibc: conversion at [38;5;10mlong[0m width, saturating at [38;5;10mi64[0m bounds, then truncated to [38;5;10mi32[0m, so [38;5;10m4294967297[0m yields [38;5;10m1[0m and [38;5;10m99999999999999999999999[0m yields [38;5;10m-1[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I did not fix the original's quirks: the unreachable-looking [38;5;10mlabel1[0m fallthrough, the ignored [38;5;10mscanf[0m return value, and the possibility of an infinite loop on inputs like [38;5;10m1 -5[0m all behave as they do in C.[0m[0m
